{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.BindingGevreyHalf","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.BindingGevreyHalf","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneC8SuperExpBound"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.BindingGevreyHalf.wstd_superexp_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.BindingGevreyHalf.wstd_superexp_sq","kind":"theorem","line":129,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/BindingGevreyHalf.lean#L129-L151","doc":"**The C8 lower bound is genuinely super-exponential (concrete form).** Plugging the\nGevrey-1/2 involution bound `I(k)² ≤ 2^k·k!` into the abstract\n`c8_superexp_sq_lower_bound` gives, for the Plancherel-weighted branched sum\n`w_std = (Σ f² g)/k!` with the SYT inputs (A) `Σ f g = k!/3` and (C) `Σ g ≤ I(k)`:\n\n  `w_std² ≥ k! / (81 · 2^k)`,\n\na genuinely **super-exponential** lower bound (`k!/2^k → ∞`), strengthening the bare\n`w_std ≥ 1/9` that `I(k) ≤ k!` alone yields. The SYT inputs (A)/(C) remain the open\ncombinatorial core (they are hypotheses here, not discharged); this only sharpens the\n*downstream bound* they feed — honestly, no fabrication of the branched identities. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.BindingGevreyHalf.involutionNumber_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.BindingGevreyHalf.involutionNumber_pos","kind":"theorem","line":117,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/BindingGevreyHalf.lean#L117-L127","doc":"`0 < I(n)` — the involution numbers are positive (needed as the `Ik > 0`\ninput to the C8 super-exponential bound). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.BindingGevreyHalf.gevrey_half_sandwich","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.BindingGevreyHalf.gevrey_half_sandwich","kind":"theorem","line":108,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/BindingGevreyHalf.lean#L108-L115","doc":"The two-sided **Gevrey-1/2 sandwich** in one statement:\n`n! ≤ 4ⁿ·I(n)²` and `I(n)² ≤ 2ⁿ·n!`, i.e. `I(n) = (n!)^{1/2}·(exp-bounded)`.\nThe binding period's classical growth order is exactly 1/2 — the level-1 Borel\nplane is singularity-free for it, and the level-1/2 plane is canonical. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.BindingGevreyHalf.factorial_le_four_pow_mul_involution_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.BindingGevreyHalf.factorial_le_four_pow_mul_involution_sq","kind":"theorem","line":76,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/BindingGevreyHalf.lean#L76-L106","doc":"**Lower half of the Gevrey-1/2 sandwich:** `n! ≤ 4ⁿ·I(n)²`.\n\nTwo-step induction using only `I(n+2) ≥ (n+1)·I(n)` (drop the `I(n+1)` term of the\ntelephone recurrence); the induction margin is `(n+2)(n+1) ≤ 16(n+1)²`.\nThis grounds the super-exponential (order exactly 1/2) growth of the period from\nbelow: `I(n) ≥ √(n!/4ⁿ)`, so `a_D^(n)` genuinely leaves every exponential class. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.BindingGevreyHalf.involution_sq_le_two_pow_mul_factorial","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.BindingGevreyHalf.involution_sq_le_two_pow_mul_factorial","kind":"theorem","line":38,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/BindingGevreyHalf.lean#L38-L74","doc":"**Upper half of the Gevrey-1/2 sandwich:** `I(n)² ≤ 2ⁿ·n!`.\n\nTwo-step induction from `I(n+2) = I(n+1) + (n+1)·I(n)` via\n`(a+b)² ≤ 2a² + 2b²`; the induction margin is `n+3 ≤ 2(n+2)`.\nThis bounds the binding period strictly below every Gevrey-1 (factorial) class:\n`I(n) ≤ √(2ⁿ n!) = o(n!·rⁿ)` for every `r > 0`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}